      "maxPoints": 1,
      "modelAnswer": "After the Russian Revolution brought Stalin to power, he launched collectivization in 1928 as part of his Five-Year Plan to rapidly industrialize the Soviet Union—abolishing private land ownership under communism, seizing control of the Ukrainian 'bread basket,' and forcing peasants onto collective farms to feed urban workers and compete with industrialized nations.",
      "explanation": "Full credit (1 point) for a correct historical circumstance behind Soviet collectivization. Acceptable responses (NYSED rating guide) include: Stalin's Five-Year Plan to increase industrial/agricultural production; the Soviet Union industrializing and needing food for city workers; the Russian Revolution leading to the rise of Stalin/totalitarianism; the Soviet Union following communism and abolishing private property; Stalin wanting to modernize/industrialize; Stalin consolidating power after Lenin's death; wanting to seize control of the Ukrainian bread basket; competition with industrialized countries; the devastation of WWI/the Russian Civil War; or viewing the New Economic Policy as too capitalist. Not acceptable: 'Stalin sent people to Siberia/the gulags,' 'the Cold War,' 'Lenin died,' 'the Great Depression,' 'peace, land, and bread,' 'Stalin gave land to the peasants,' 'Ukraine was the bread basket,' or 'a program called dekulakization.'",
      "image": "/images/exams/global-history/january-2026/context_29_29.png",
      "context": "Base your answer to question 29 on Document 1 below and on your knowledge of social studies.\\nDocument 1\\n. . .By the end of the 1920s, Joseph Stalin had consolidated power as the unchallenged \\nleader of the Soviet Union. In 1928 he introduced a program to collectivize farms and end \\nprivate ownership of land. Farmers in Ukraine, known as the “bread basket” of the Soviet \\nUnion for its fertile soil, resisted giving up their land to the state and joining collective \\nfarms. \\nThe Soviet state under Stalin responded with a program called “dekulakization,” \\nwhich evicted farmers and their families. The word “kulak” means “fi st” in Russian and \\nwas used to demonize private farmers who refused to open their “clenched fi sts” and \\nparticipate in building socialism. More than a million Ukrainian farmers were exiled to \\nthe far reaches of the Soviet Union or left without homes or work in the process. . . .\\nSource: “Collectivization and the Holodomor,” The Henry M. Jackson School of International Studies, June 2017"
